WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.420
There's a saying amongst miners

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:07.290
that if it can't be
grown, it must be mined.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.607
And this is absolutely true for the metals

00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:13.370
and the materials that we
need for modern lifestyles.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.700
Demand for metals, for all kinds of metals

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.720
is rising very strongly.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.540
Simply because economies are reopening

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.840
from the nadir of Covid-19.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.050
That really boosts demand
for all sorts of things.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.900
Your household stuff,

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.930
your refrigerators, including big things

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.550
like if we are building a new bridge

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.580
or building a new highway,

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.150
they all need metals.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.943
So demand for metals is
really rising strongly.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.780
Nickel mining in particular
has been increasing in demand

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.650
since the early 2000s,

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.330
largely due to China's economic boom.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.130
And the rise of electric vehicles

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.750
means nickel consumption
is only set to grow.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.120
Meeting demand for nickel

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.060
means that nickel mining needs to upscale

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.657
in many countries around the world

00:01:00.657 --> 00:01:02.650
and it means expanding nickel mines

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:04.890
using traditional methods of strip mining,

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.263
which will have an effect environmentally.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.760
And so with increasing demand

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.550
comes greater responsibility

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.170
to minimize the damage caused by mining,

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.413
and to search for sustainable options.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.420
For the past 30 years,
researchers worldwide

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.140
have been pursuing a more radical

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.100
and eco-friendly technology
called agromining.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.423
Also known as phytomining.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:30.180
Growing crops of plants
that absorb and store metal

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.040
from the soil, that can then be harvested.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.470
We have already worked with zinc,

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:38.730
with the rare earth elements,
which are very important

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.320
for recent technologies.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.980
And it's also possible to recover cobalt

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.010
from this technology.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.500
But despite the success of this research,

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.300
does agromining stand a chance

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.240
against the massive scale
of conventional mining?

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.410
And it can convince a legacy
industry to change its ways?

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:57.940
I think they are committed.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.070
I think they have really good intentions

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:04.590
to mine their products
in a sustainable way,

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:08.653
but ultimately, I think
mining is a dirty business.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.328
While mining is necessary and
has many positive benefits,

00:02:21.328 --> 00:02:22.961
must say that,

00:02:22.961 --> 00:02:24.910
it does have many negative aspects,

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:28.020
and especially the impacts
on the environment.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.770
Mine wastes are large sort of volume

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.060
of waste material generated by mining

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.230
and these can be in liquid form

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:41.000
and they can contain very acidic waters

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.640
that contain a lot of metals
and metalloids that are toxic.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.980
And so when those get out
into the water environment,

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.090
they can contaminate land and water,

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.963
kill plants, reduce biodiversity.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.960
Mining is also responsible
for air pollution,

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.490
from the dust generated during extraction

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.450
and the roasting and burning of ores,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.940
which can lead to acid rain

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.760
as well as contribute to emissions.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.590
Surface mining also causes deforestation

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.403
as trees are cleared to
make space for mining sites.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.990
Nickel mining in particular
presents problems

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:16.440
due to its emissions.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.790
To the extent that in 2020,
Elon Musk put out a call

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.950
for ways to meet nickel
demand sustainably.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.510
You may think steel is
the most polluting metal

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.900
when it comes to CO2 emissions,

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.960
but actually nickel has the
highest CO2 emission intensity

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:33.910
of all metals.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:37.100
CO2 emissions per ton of metal

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.320
for the nickel production on average

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.960
is about 18 tons of CO2 per ton of metal.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.560
Nickel processing, smelting and refining

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.740
also has the highest CO2
emissions of mined metals.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.690
I would say probably the
actual extraction of minerals

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:55.380
and metals and materials,

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.660
does the most damage.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:58.850
If you have a deposit

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:02.590
that maybe contains 1% copper these days,

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.700
that means 90% is waste material.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.650
And so you have to clear that

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.660
to get to the 1% copper that you need.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:12.760
So it's probably the extraction phase

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.150
that generates the most damage,

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:18.600
and the most waste, which
therefore needs to be managed.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.110
The mining industry is
estimated to make up

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.773
4% to 7% of greenhouse
gas emissions worldwide.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.360
But mining companies are
facing increased scrutiny

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.410
from investors and regulators

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:35.300
who want them to pay attention
to environmental issues,

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:37.683
and commit to carbon reduction targets.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.640
The nickel industry is making improvements

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.640
to make mining more
environmentally friendly.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:49.040
For example, some plants have
been using renewable energy

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.250
hybrid systems as a fuel source.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:56.780
And Indonesia, they are the
largest producer in the world,

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.060
they also set up emission targets

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:01.060
for the next decade.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.320
But the industry as a whole is slow

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:04.973
compared to other industries.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.920
But the global demand for metals

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.480
needed by modern society,

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.350
means destructive mining
practices will continue,

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.223
if not expand in the future.

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.360
But in the east of France,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:20.600
there's a group of scientists

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.803
hoping to speed up the pace of change.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:25.810
Dr. Antony van der Ent is visiting

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.210
from the University of Queensland.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:30.990
For the past 15 years,
he's been investigating

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:33.180
the power of plants to harvest minerals

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.990
and metals from the soil.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:37.960
And along with scientists
from across Europe and Asia,

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:41.396
he's been developing a
process called agromining.

00:05:41.396 --> 00:05:44.220
So agromining is the process of growing

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.810
hyperaccumulator plants
on an agricultural scale,

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.210
in a type of agricultural operation.

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:53.422
And not to produce something
that you can eat, food crop,

00:05:53.422 --> 00:05:57.260
but to harvest the metal
from their biomass.

00:05:58.116 --> 00:06:01.040
So hyperaccumulators are
a rare group of plants

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.190
that have the unusual ability

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:05.830
to accumulate very high concentrations

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.270
of particular metals
into their living shoots,

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:09.700
so into their leaves.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.370
We know of about 700 of them

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.410
that occur all around the world.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:17.110
And most of them are known
for nickel, 500 or so of them.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.980
We know hyperaccumulators
for a whole range

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:20.250
of different metals,

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:21.940
including thallium and zinc,

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:25.030
and copper, cobalt, manganese.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.270
But we keep discovering
more of these plants

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:28.640
wherever we do research.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:32.450
So it is about 350,000 plant
species around the world,

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:34.870
and we think there are
more hyperaccumulators

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.373
that are awaiting discovery.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.430
A nickel hyperaccumulator
plant takes up the metal

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:43.140
present in the soil through its roots.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:46.283
It then stores it in the skin
of its leaves or biomass.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.970
After the plants are harvested,
this biomass is dried

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:51.093
and incinerated.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:54.380
The ash created by burning the plants

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:55.970
is then ready to be processed

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:57.530
to create a bio ore,

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:59.480
from which the nickel can be recovered.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:05.630
Chemical engineering professor,
Marie-Odile Simonnot,

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:07.550
uses a form of hydrometallurgy

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:10.127
to extract the nickel from the bio ore.

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:13.370
So here is the ash,

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:15.680
and the ash is very rich in nickel.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.720
It contains up to 20% of nickel,

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.273
that is more than any ore on the Earth.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:27.293
Then nickel is extracted from the ash.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.823
The ash is first washed.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.290
And then nickel is extracted by an acid

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:37.133
at a high temperature.

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:45.630
And this solution is
filtered to remove the ash

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:48.823
and recover the solution
containing nickel.

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:55.340
And finally, we have a precipitation stage

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:58.900
in which nickel is precipitated

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.530
in the form of a nickel salt for instance.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:04.633
But we could do any
kind of nickel compound.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:09.190
It could possibly function
for many other metals,

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:13.800
depending on we have plants
able to accumulate the metals

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:16.740
and develop the methods
to recover the metals

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:18.000
from the plants.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.050
But the first step is to have the plants.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.880
Nearby, at Econick's experimental farm,

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.690
professor Guillaume Echevarria

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:30.460
and his team grow new varieties

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:32.370
of hyperaccumulator plants

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:34.203
to be trialed around the world.

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:41.630
So here we are in the
Econick experimental farm.

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:45.960
This is where we prepare
seedlings and plants

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.010
to transplant into contaminated soils.

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:52.860
But also this is where we
try to breed new varieties

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.180
of hyperaccumulators to grow in Greece

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:56.303
and other countries.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:01.890
This is Sedum plumbizincicola,

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:03.560
so it bears the name of the metals

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.190
where it was found.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:08.440
This is a zinc and
cadmium hyperaccumulator,

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:10.920
so we mostly use it to decontaminate

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:12.590
contaminated soils.

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:14.820
This is Pelargonium capitatum.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.800
And this is a lead hyperaccumulator,

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:22.130
again, to decontaminate polluted soils.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:25.906
Here we have the local zinc
and cadmium hyperaccumulator,

00:09:25.906 --> 00:09:28.350
Noccaea caerulescens,

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:31.103
which is endemic from Western Europe.

00:09:32.548 --> 00:09:34.240
Guillaume and his team carefully breed

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.860
and select plants to improve
nickel extraction from the soil

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.900
with the goal to double
the amount of metal

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:41.623
a plant can absorb and store.

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:44.323
So this plant

00:09:44.323 --> 00:09:46.690
is the most efficient
nickel hyperaccumulator

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:48.090
we have in Europe.

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:52.720
So leaves can contain up to
4% of nickel, dry weight.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:55.528
That means when we ash them,

00:09:55.528 --> 00:09:58.986
we can reach 29, 30%

00:09:58.986 --> 00:10:01.230
of nickel in the ashes.

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:02.130
That's the record.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.950
While incinerating the plants uses energy,

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:11.010
the team's tests have found
the overall agromining process

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:13.150
uses significantly less energy

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.173
than conventional mining procedures.

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:20.730
These highly efficient
hyperaccumulator plants

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:24.040
are also able to grow in
soil which is ultramafic,

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:25.680
containing high levels of metals

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:28.070
that can be naturally
occurring through rocks

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:30.300
or contamination from human activities,

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.280
including mining.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:34.611
Traditional strip mining
would also take place

00:10:34.611 --> 00:10:38.430
on land that has the very
highest grade of nickel.

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:41.220
In contrast, agromining
can take place on soils

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:43.090
that are much lower in nickel,

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:44.400
which are very widespread.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.880
So for example, in Indonesia,

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:50.160
the island of Sulawesi has
over 15,000 square kilometers

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:51.920
of ultramafic soils.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:53.980
Now perhaps only a
small percentage of that

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:54.900
is suitable for agromining,

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:57.390
but that still represents a huge area

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:59.070
that could be developed.

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:01.300
Now the impact from agromining

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:04.420
is comparable to normal,
other types of agriculture.

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:06.860
So it has a very low
environmental footprint.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:08.920
That contrasts quite
strongly to strip mining

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:09.883
in that respect.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:13.910
Ultramafic soils usually lack

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:16.520
the essential nutrients
needed to grow most crops,

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:19.760
so they're abandoned by
conventional agriculture.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:21.450
One of the more immediate benefits

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:23.240
of hyperaccumulator plants

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.290
is that they can help to
clean up contaminated soils,

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:29.450
provided their roots can reach the toxins.

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:31.730
Installing the hyperaccumulator plants

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:33.520
on this kind of material

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:36.350
is a kind of nature based solution,

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.560
which allows the vegetation

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:41.150
and the soil fertility to grow quicker

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.380
than if you apply conventional methods

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:45.033
of soil rehabilitation.

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:48.770
Agromining, especially
in the case of nickel,

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:51.700
takes place in regions
that have poor soils

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:53.980
because of their lack of fertility.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:56.730
It can really be an
opportunity for small farmers

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:58.620
to earn a living.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.482
Then of course, it's very interesting

00:12:00.482 --> 00:12:03.960
to provide both renewable
energy opportunities

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:07.720
and also the production
of a very targeted mineral

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:10.023
or even metallurgical industry.

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:12.600
We think there's a lot of potential

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.610
for agromining to be undertaken
by local communities,

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:18.740
particularly in the
Philippines and Indonesia.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:21.620
Where local communities could
grow hyperaccumulator plants

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:25.280
much like any other crop,
on ultramafic soils.

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:26.730
Those soils, ultramafic soils

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:29.820
are typically not suitable
for normal food crops

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:31.100
because they're highly infertile

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:33.500
and because the nickel
in the soil is toxic.

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:36.380
So over a time that the
phytomining takes place,

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:39.130
which we think is between 20 and 30 years,

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:40.590
the soil quality is improved

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:42.360
by increasing the fertility

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:45.430
and by decreasing the nickel
that is in the soil overtime,

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:48.730
so at the end of the phytomining
or agromining process,

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:50.920
the soil is actually suitable potentially

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:52.583
for normal food crops.

00:12:54.891 --> 00:12:57.330
There have already been
successful commercial trials

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:59.610
in Europe for smaller scale farming.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:01.120
But the future of agromining

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:04.253
depends on collaboration with
commercial mining companies.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:07.670
We don't think that agromining

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:11.530
can replace traditional
mining of nickel entirely.

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.910
Traditional mining takes
place at huge scale,

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:16.200
it has very high efficiency of processing,

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:17.930
enormous quantities of ore,

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:20.620
talking kilotons of
nickel in a year's time

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:21.860
for a single mine.

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:23.600
So we see this as a supplementary form

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:25.160
of producing nickel

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:26.840
with a very low environmental impact

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:28.913
alongside the traditional mining

00:13:28.913 --> 00:13:31.623
that needs to supply nickel at scale.

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:34.630
So typically, on a mine lease,

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:38.410
only a small part of the
whole site will be mined,

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:41.744
but as a much larger area that
still has nickel in the soil

00:13:41.744 --> 00:13:45.590
which is below the current
grade for normal processing.

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:48.700
So in that halo around
the mining operation,

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:50.500
you could also develop agromining

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:53.640
as a complementary technique

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:55.880
alongside the traditional mining.

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:56.750
The challenge of course

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:59.110
is to make an economic business case

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:01.100
where the scale at which this could happen

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:03.820
is large enough and the
product, the bio ore,

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:06.020
valuable enough to make
it worth the effort.

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:09.720
Their technology does look really cool,

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:11.050
really appealing,

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:13.680
but they are all at very early stage.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:17.710
They have not reached to
mass production level.

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:19.410
For example, for nickel,

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:22.850
we all know that we are going
to need a lot more nickel

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:23.683
in the future,

00:14:23.683 --> 00:14:26.210
to be able to achieve the climate goals

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:29.660
pledged by automakers, governments

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:31.690
and just like the world in general,

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:33.710
to build a better future.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:37.340
If those technologies are
cool, are really valid

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:42.150
and legit, they need to
really kind of work out

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:43.580
mass production.

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:47.270
If they get endorsements
from those big automakers

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:50.030
and OEM's, then that to me

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:51.100
is a big thing.

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:52.470
That means they are on the map,

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:54.923
that will attract more investors.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:01.840
It's taken a few years for agromining

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:03.230
to find its place

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:05.130
and to be fully validated.

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:07.590
The main challenge has been to essentially

00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:09.900
get it from a scientific idea

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.830
to test it in the lab
scale and in the greenhouse

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:14.760
and then to bring it to the fields.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:16.830
Many other species still remain untested

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:19.670
even though we think
there's a lot of potential,

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:20.520
not just for nickel

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:23.690
but also for other elements
that can be agromined,

00:15:23.690 --> 00:15:25.220
including cobalt and manganese

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:26.330
and zinc and selenium.

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:28.270
A lot of that has to do with essentially

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:29.930
finding the right species,

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:32.330
and then finding funding to set up trials,

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:33.320
both in the lab

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:35.113
and then upgrade it to the field.

00:15:36.666 --> 00:15:39.230
Agromining is of course
one of the different tools

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:41.058
we will need in the future,

00:15:41.058 --> 00:15:43.710
besides circular economy, recycling

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:46.750
and of course, exploiting new resources.

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:47.990
So I think in the future,

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:50.560
it would be great that we can benefit

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:53.010
from this knowledge that has been acquired

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:54.750
for decades now.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:58.560
And really use the potential
of these hyperaccumulator crops

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:01.910
to really produce the tremendous amounts

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:03.050
of strategic metals

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:06.187
that we will need for the
ecological transition.

00:16:06.187 --> 00:16:09.190
I think for environmentalists,

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:11.570
they need to be aware of that.

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:15.320
It's a very delicate
balancing act for miners.

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:18.090
On the one hand, they need
to produce the products

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:22.260
we actually need in the
future and a lot of them.

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:25.290
But on the other hand,
they also need to be aware

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:27.283
of the environmental impact.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:29.870
So governments are very interested

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:31.800
in these types of techniques

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:34.550
to extract metals and reduce toxicity

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:36.423
and reduce the environmental impacts.

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:40.530
It very much fits in the
circular economy theme

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:43.500
of putting materials back into the system

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:44.900
and reusing them,

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:46.440
rather than just generating waste

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:48.580
or having to dig up new materials

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:50.110
and I think we'll see much more of it

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:51.160
in the years to come.

